1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,170
Four dates on a Medicare calendar.

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:05,590
Miss the first one and you keep
a plan you didn't choose.

3
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:08,790
Miss the last one and the
health questions come back.

4
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:10,010
Miss the one in the middle…

5
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:15,750
and the penalty is ten percent, on every
premium, for as long as you have Medicare.

6
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:20,770
At today's rate, two years late is about
four hundred eighty-seven dollars a year…

7
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:22,710
for the rest of your life.

8
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:25,250
This is The Fine Print.

9
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:30,910
Four dates, read line by line: what each
one lets you do, what it costs to miss it,

10
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:33,670
and the one place to write
them down so you don't.

11
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:35,950
This is education, not advice.

12
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:37,770
Nobody here sells insurance.

13
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,470
Start with the date that's closest.

14
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:42,190
December seventh.

15
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:47,090
That's the end of the annual enrollment period,
the window that opened October fifteenth.

16
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:52,210
Until then, anyone on Medicare can join,
switch, or drop a Medicare Advantage plan,

17
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:54,750
and join or change a drug plan.

18
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:59,380
After December seventh, the plan you didn't
change is the plan you keep for twenty

19
00:00:59,430 --> 00:01:00,230
twenty-seven.

20
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:03,370
And twenty twenty-seven
is not twenty twenty-six.

21
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:07,850
The most a drug plan can charge as a deductible
goes from six hundred fifteen dollars

22
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:09,590
to seven hundred.

23
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,910
The yearly cap on what you pay for covered
drugs goes from twenty-one hundred to

24
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:14,810
twenty-four hundred.

25
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:19,330
Those are Medicare's ceilings; your plan's
own numbers are in the letter it mailed

26
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:20,410
you in September…

27
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:22,470
the Annual Notice of Change.

28
00:01:22,670 --> 00:01:27,950
If a drug moved to a higher tier, or the
premium moved, that's where it says so.

29
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:29,370
How to check yours.

30
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:30,910
Find that letter.

31
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:35,050
Page two or three has a table:
this year next to next year.

32
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:37,230
Circle every line that went up.

33
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:41,770
If more than one did, open the plan finder
on Medicare dot gov with your drug list

34
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:43,310
before the seventh.

35
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:45,790
Now January first.

36
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:46,990
The quiet date.

37
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:49,090
Whatever you chose takes effect.

38
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:54,030
The deductibles reset to the twenty twenty-seven
figures, including the Part B deductible

39
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:55,850
Medicare announces this month.

40
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:59,170
And one clock starts that most
people never hear about.

41
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:03,770
If you dropped a Medicare supplement to try
Medicare Advantage for the first time…

42
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:06,710
January first starts a twelve-month trial.

43
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:10,810
Inside those twelve months you can go back
to Original Medicare and get your old

44
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:14,650
supplement back, or buy certain
others, with no health questions.

45
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:19,710
On day three hundred sixty-six, in most
states, the questions come back.

46
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:21,350
How to check yours.

47
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:26,310
Write the date your Advantage plan started
on the card, and the date one year later.

48
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:28,710
That second date is the one that matters.

49
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:31,510
March thirty-first.

50
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:35,450
Two windows close on the same day,
and they're easy to mix up.

51
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:40,690
The first is the Medicare Advantage open
enrollment period, January first to March

52
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:41,950
thirty-first.

53
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,810
If you're in an Advantage
plan and it isn't working…

54
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:49,070
the doctor isn't in the network, the copays
in the chart weren't what the brochure

55
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:49,920
said…

56
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:51,610
you get one change.

57
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:52,560
One.

58
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:57,110
You can move to a different Advantage plan,
or go back to Original Medicare and pick

59
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:58,270
up a drug plan.

60
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:00,950
Coverage starts the first
of the next month.

61
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:05,790
What you can't do in this window is go the
other way, from Original Medicare into

62
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:09,990
Advantage, and it doesn't come with a supplement
guarantee unless one of the other

63
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:11,230
rules covers you.

64
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:15,310
The second window is the general
enrollment period, same dates.

65
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,070
That's for people who should have
signed up for Part B and didn't…

66
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:22,410
often because they thought retiree
coverage or COBRA counted.

67
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:23,730
It doesn't.

68
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:27,810
Sign up between January and March,
coverage starts the month after.

69
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:31,430
And here's the line that costs
the most on the whole calendar.

70
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:33,530
The Part B late penalty.

71
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:37,770
Ten percent of the premium for every twelve
months you could have had Part B and

72
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:38,530
didn't.

73
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:39,650
Not for a year.

74
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:41,670
For as long as you have Medicare.

75
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:42,550
Run it.

76
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:47,250
The twenty twenty-six premium is two hundred
two dollars and ninety cents a month.

77
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:49,370
Two years late is twenty percent…

78
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:52,250
about forty dollars and fifty-eight
cents a month…

79
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:54,690
four hundred eighty-seven dollars a year.

80
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:59,560
Hold the premium flat, which it won't be,
and twenty years of Medicare is about nine

81
00:03:59,610 --> 00:04:01,030
thousand seven hundred dollars…

82
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,410
for a form you didn't file.

83
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:08,010
That's nearly ten months of a thousand-dollar-a-month
draw from savings, gone to

84
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:08,860
a date.

85
00:04:09,060 --> 00:04:11,030
Nobody sends a bill for the penalty.

86
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:15,850
It just lives inside the premium,
every month, and you stop noticing.

87
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:17,290
How to check yours.

88
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:22,250
If you're sixty-five and still working, or
on a spouse's plan, ask the benefits office

89
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:27,490
one question in writing: is this coverage from
an employer with twenty or more employees?

90
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:30,730
If the answer is no, Part B can't wait.

91
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:34,610
The fourth date isn't on the calendar.

92
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:37,110
It's a count: sixty-three days.

93
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:42,030
When a plan leaves Medicare, or leaves your
county, or you move out of its area,

94
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:44,610
you get a guaranteed right
to buy a supplement…

95
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:45,870
Plan G among them…

96
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:48,590
with no health questions, and no waiting.

97
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:53,670
The right runs from sixty days before your
coverage ends to sixty-three days after.

98
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:58,950
Day sixty-four, in most states, an insurer
can ask the questions again, charge more,

99
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:00,210
or say no.

100
00:05:00,540 --> 00:05:04,030
Last year about two point nine
million people got that letter.

101
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:07,410
The letter is the key,
and the key expires.

102
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:08,770
How to check yours.

103
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:14,190
If a letter says your plan is ending, write
the date coverage ends on the card and

104
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:16,270
count sixty-three days forward.

105
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:17,880
Apply before that.

106
00:05:19,660 --> 00:05:20,490
Four dates.

107
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:22,410
December seventh.

108
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:23,970
January first.

109
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:24,840
March thirty-first.

110
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:28,770
And sixty-three days from a letter
you hope you never get.

111
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:33,390
Every one of them moves what comes out of
savings next year, and none of them sends

112
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:34,240
a reminder.

113
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:36,190
So put the card where you'll see it.

114
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:40,690
The four dates, your plan's start
date, and one year after it.

115
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,850
A fee-only fiduciary or a CPA can run your
own numbers, and the State Health Insurance

116
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:49,210
Assistance Program will go through
the calendar with you for free.

117
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:51,650
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

118
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,230
Subscribe so the next one finds you.

119
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:57,590
Will your money last as long as you do?
